  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is <b>AutoScalingRollingUpdate</b>. This method performs updates in batches, launching new instances with the desired configuration while gradually terminating the old ones. By setting 'MinInstancesInService' to 2, you guarantee that at least two instances stay operational throughout the update process, preventing any downtime.</p><p><i>AutoScalingReplacingUpdate</i> is not suitable in this scenario as it replaces all instances at once, which can cause a brief outage if all existing instances are terminated before new ones are available. <i>AutoScalingIntegrationUpdate</i> is not an actual deployment strategy provided by AWS, making it invalid. <i>AutoScalingScheduledAction</i> is generally used for pre-scheduling changes to your group's size or capacity and doesn’t facilitate controlled rolling updates or guarantee minimum instances stay running during an update.</p><p><b>Tip:</b> Use <b>AutoScalingRollingUpdate</b> whenever you require zero downtime and need to maintain a required number of healthy instances during updates. Always verify your deployment settings to meet your availability goals.</p>",
  "query": "<p>Your production environment relies on an Auto Scaling Group with four instances. You want to switch to a different instance type, but it's vital that at least two instances always remain available, and there’s no service downtime. Which of the following methods ensures a seamless, interruption-free update?</p>",
